{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And Reuben returned, etc.</b> One needs to know what Reuben's claim against his brothers was when he said <b>and as for me, where [shall I go]</b>, etc. — for by his own counsel too Joseph was lost in the pit. And although the verse states (Genesis 37:22) ״לְמַעַן הַצִּיל״ etc. — to restore him to his father, this was in his heart, not in his mouth to his brothers; and the proof is that Judah said (Genesis 37:26) ״מַה בֶּצַע כִּי נַהֲרֹג אֶת אָחִינוּ״ etc. after he had already been in the pit. From this you learn that casting him into the pit was for killing, as we explained above. If so, what answer is there in his saying ״the child [is not], and as for me,״ etc.?" "<b>Indeed,</b> Reuben's intention is this: since he was the firstborn, his father would press him to go search for him from one end of the world to the other. And had he been in the pit, he would have brought him out dead from the creatures in the pit, and he would have reported to his father, ״Here he is dead; an evil beast devoured him״ — which is not the case now, for where shall he go amid the winds of the world to seek him? And this is what he meant by <b>and as for me, where shall I go</b>. Therefore they devised a plan and slaughtered a goat of the goats, etc., and by this Reuben's complaint was set aside."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:51.379433330Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}